# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repo stores LF and every deploy target is Linux (Docker, Caddy, the VPS).
# Without this file, git's core.autocrlf was unset while something on Windows
# wrote CRLF into the working tree — so 18 untouched files showed as modified
# on every `git status`, and a real change was ~1,100 lines of noise away from
# being readable.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: detect text, normalise to LF on commit, check out
# LF. A CRLF working-tree file whose LF form matches the index no longer counts
# as modified, so the churn stops whichever way an editor saves.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native tooling. PowerShell handles LF fine but the ISE and some
# signing tools do not, and these are only ever run on Arthur's machine.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Must be LF regardless of platform — CRLF breaks a shebang line ("bad
# interpreter: /bin/bash^M") and Caddy/Docker parsing.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.Caddyfile   text eol=lf
Caddyfile     text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*  text eol=lf
*.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.zip  binary

# Generated / vendored — keep them out of diffs and language stats.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
